DOLLY 7
PACKAGE ps
REQUIRES HOST runtime@0

# ps prints the kernel's list of processes, /proc/processes, under a heading.
# A script: every image with a shell has Slop, echo and cat.
FILE /usr/share/man/cat1/ps.1
    usage: ps
    List the processes that have not exited, one a line: PID, the parent's
    PID, state and command line. A process is starting until its program
    runs, then running. A control character of the command line shows as ?,
    and a line ends after 2047 bytes.
    The lines are the kernel's /proc/processes, which any program can open
    and read. ps takes no options.
FILE /bin/ps
    #!/bin/slop
    case "$*" in
      '') echo 'PID PPID STATE COMMAND' && cat /proc/processes ;;
      --help) cat /usr/share/man/cat1/ps.1 ;;
      *) cat /usr/share/man/cat1/ps.1 >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
EXPORTS TOOL ps
